WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.982
This query sequences admissions within each patient.

00:00:02.982 --> 00:00:06.137
LAG retrieves the previous discharge time.

00:00:06.137 --> 00:00:10.387
The outer query subtracts that previous discharge from the current admit time.

00:00:10.387 --> 00:00:13.102
The result is the interval between encounters.

00:00:13.102 --> 00:00:19.117
The first admission for each patient has no previous discharge, so it is excluded in the final stage.

00:00:19.117 --> 00:00:23.897
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:23.897 --> 00:00:29.802
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:29.802 --> 00:00:32.017
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:37.362
In a real setting such as an operations team comparing each event with the previous and next event,

00:00:37.362 --> 00:00:42.967
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:45.002
before discussing syntax.

00:00:45.002 --> 00:00:50.480
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
